<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">Cofitachequi</h1><p class="st-meta">1300 CE – 1670 CE (approx.) · capital Cofitachequi</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Mississippian chiefdom in South Carolina, famed for the woman ruler who received Hernando de Soto with gifts of freshwater pearls in 1540.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Cofitachequi arose among Mississippian farming towns on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wateree-river">Wateree River</a> in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, with its main centre probably at the Mulberry mound site near modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/camden-south-carolina">Camden</a>. By the 14th century it had become the leading power of the region, and its paramount chief claimed authority over towns across much of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-carolina">Carolina</a> piedmont and coastal plain.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>In 1540 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernando-de-soto">Hernando de Soto's</a> army was met by a woman ruler, known to the Spanish as the Lady of Cofitachequi, who presented strings of pearls; the Spanish looted pearls from a temple-burial house and took her hostage, though she escaped. Spanish accounts describe towns with mounds, granaries of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a> and stored deerskins and textiles. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-pardo-explorer">Juan Pardo</a> passed through in 1566 to 1568.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Epidemics and the Spanish and later English <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> weakened the chiefdom. When the English trader <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/henry-woodward-colonist">Henry Woodward</a> visited in 1670 it was still recognised but much smaller, and it vanished soon after, its people probably merging with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catawba-people">Catawba</a> and other piedmont groups.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/cofitachequi.jpg" alt="Cofitachequi" width="320" height="161"><figcaption>Image: The Hsinging Tree · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Talimeco_Mound.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1300 CE – 1670 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Cofitachequi</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Mississippian religion</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Cofitachequi chiefdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1485&amp;focus=cofitachequi" aria-label="Cofitachequi on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2262 760 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Cofitachequi lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2582 1016h0"/><path class="map-dot-ap" d="M2582 1016h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>Location approximate: shown in present-day United States. <a href="/atlas?year=1485&amp;focus=cofitachequi">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

